# Epic plan file

The approved execution contract. {% if harness.id == "codex" or harness.id == "antigravity" %}Write and validate it in step 0:{% else %}Draft it in a step-0 plan-mode session:{% endif %} the wave lead plans every epic in a wave; the epic lead plans a standalone run. The wave lead sets the cross-epic path and binding envelope. Epic leads adapt execution inside that envelope, including standalone execution. Task authority never grants host, sandbox, filesystem, or network permissions.

State outcomes, acceptance, owned scope, contracts, dependencies, resource limits, and delegated authority. Name prerequisites, coherent assignments, verification owners, required evidence and gates, and approved fallbacks. Leave local grouping, independent-step ordering, minor sizing overruns, and implementation choices to the epic lead and workers. Each milestone still needs a fresh worker and independent review.

The front matter is validated mechanically: `bun run ~/.{% if harness.id == "antigravity" %}gemini/antigravity-cli{% else %}{{ harness.id }}{% endif %}/skills/constitution-tooling/scripts/cli.ts check-plan <file> --root .` checks it against `constitution-tooling/assets/schemas/epic-plan.schema.json` and cross-checks the epic file, the register, and the sibling epics. Run it {% if harness.id == "grok" %}after the repo plan file is written, and after every amendment. Plan mode cannot write that file: `check-plan` runs after `exit_plan_mode`.{% else %}before presenting the plan and after every amendment.{% endif %}

## Location

Outside `.constitution/`, always. The orchestrator's write ban on the constitution is what keeps `reconcile` trustworthy, and the plan is a session artifact, not a record. The recommended path is `.{% if harness.id == "antigravity" %}agents{% else %}{{ harness.id }}{% endif %}/plans/EPIC-<ID>.md` in the main checkout, gitignored, passed to the epic lead as an absolute path. A project may name another location in its agent instructions.{% if harness.id == "grok" %} The session `plan.md` under `~/.grok/sessions/...` is only the plan-mode draft.{% endif %}

## Shape

YAML front matter carries mechanical fields; the body carries binding constraints and execution state. Existing plans remain valid without the optional `invocation` and `reasoning_effort` fields.{% if harness.id == "codex" %} Codex and Claude can be selected independently for every role. For mixed runtimes, read [Coordinate Codex and Claude](~/.codex/skills/constitution-tooling/references/codex-claude-bridge.md); retain each role's runtime, native lifecycle handle, inbox identity, reporting route, and expected continuation in execution state. A bridge identity is a messaging destination, not a native Codex parent handle.{% endif %} New plans state invocation explicitly; on legacy-plan dispatch, supply those facts in the prompt. Use `none` where a required field doesn't apply.

```yaml
---
epic: EPIC-AD                          # FAST-<slug> or ISSUE-<number> when kind is fast_lane or no_constitution
epic_file: .constitution/tasks/epics/EPIC-AD-work-planning-foundation.yaml   # the literal none when there is no epic file
kind: epic                             # epic | fast_lane | no_constitution
planned_by: wave_lead                  # wave_lead | self (standalone)
invocation:
  mode: orchestrated                   # standalone | orchestrated | single_epic_wave
  caller: "execute-wave: <confirmed handle>"
  effective_authority: "Binding envelope and Step-0 rulings below"
  stop_boundary: "Epic report, or a policy escalation; no merge or reconcile"
wave: 1                                # 0 when standalone
merge_position: 1                      # position in the wave's listed merge order; 1 when standalone
lanes_in_wave: 3                       # 1 when standalone
worktree: {% if harness.id == "grok" %}/abs/path/worktrees/EPIC-AD{% elif harness.id == "antigravity" %}/abs/path/.agents/worktrees/EPIC-AD{% else %}/abs/path/.{{ harness.id }}/worktrees/EPIC-AD{% endif %}
branch: epic/AD-work-planning-foundation
base_branch: main                    # replace with the project's recorded default branch
planned_at_commit: 0b37d5f            # default-branch commit the repository_state assertions were checked against
siblings:                              # every other lane in the wave; empty list when standalone or the wave is one
  - epic: EPIC-AK
    lead: epic-lead-ak                 # {% if harness.id == "claude" %}that lead's background session name, which is also its SendMessage address{% elif harness.id == "codex" %}the native child label or Claude session name; record the sibling's runtime and reachable native handle or bridge address in execution state{% elif harness.id == "antigravity" %}a label for plans and reports; leads message each other with send_message using the conversationId the wave lead records in Amendments after dispatch{% else %}a label for plans and reports, not an address; leads message each other by the subagent id the wave lead records in Amendments after dispatch{% endif %}
    branch: epic/AK-notification-delivery
    merge_position: 2
models:                                # available approved models or approved inheritance; don't invent tool options
  epic_lead: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <lead model> }{% if harness.id == "codex" %} # optional reasoning_effort uses the actual runtime schema{% endif %}
  worker: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <worker model> }
  spec_patch_worker: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <spec-patch model> }
  milestone_reviewer: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <reviewer model> }
  fix_worker: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <fix-worker model> }
review_panel:                          # add a second independent reviewer for broad or high-risk changes
  - { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <reviewer model>, mode: full }
pr_review:
  fix_worker: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <fix-worker model> }
  review_escalate_at: 4
  stop: one verification round with no new Act-on finding
autonomy:
  policy:                              # what the lead does instead of asking; the defaults are shown
    - { trigger: blocker, action: adopt_recommendation }
    - { trigger: open_decision, action: adopt_recommendation }
    - { trigger: plan_drift, action: escalate_wave_lead }   # binding/shared change only; amend_plan when self or authorized single_epic_wave
    - { trigger: spec_drift, action: amend_plan }           # physical drift becomes a spec-patch milestone
    - { trigger: test_failure, action: retry, max_attempts: 3 }
    - { trigger: review_nonconvergence, action: escalate_strategy }
    - { trigger: rebase_conflict, action: resolve_and_rereview }
    - { trigger: budget_threshold, action: switch_to_fallback }
    - { trigger: stall, action: retry, max_attempts: 1 }
budget:
  usage_ceiling_note: "Record the user's stated ceiling, or no explicit ceiling"
  threshold_pct: 70
  fallback_models:                     # approved replacements for budget pressure or model unavailability
    worker: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <fallback worker model> }
    fix_worker: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <fallback fix-worker model> }
    milestone_reviewer: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <fallback reviewer model> }
    pr_reviewer: { runtime: {{ harness.id }}, model: <fallback reviewer model> }
liveness:
  stall_after_minutes: 45              # no side effect for this long means the lane is stalled
  side_effects: [commit, push, worktree_change, pr_update, plan_amendment]
verbatim_project_rules: []              # legacy field; quote only essential constraints, use focused pointers below
open_decisions:                        # register entries that touch this epic, with the interim assumption if any
  - { id: OD-12, effect: "ABC-AD003 waits", interim: none }
---
```

### Autonomy actions

| Action | What the lead does |
| :--- | :--- |
| `adopt_recommendation` | Writes the question in register shape in the epic's `decisions` list with its recommendation, marks it `interim` with the places it was applied, and continues. Never writes `prd/`, `architecture/`, or `tech-spec/`, never touches paths outside the epic's scope, never takes an irreversible action; a case that would needs `defer_tickets` instead. |
| `defer_tickets` | Sets the affected tickets to `deferred` with `deferred_to` and the reason, and continues with the rest. |
| `retry` | Diagnose the failure and retry up to `max_attempts`. Batch related repairs; after two same-premise failures, state and test the premise before another patch. Use approved availability fallbacks or a focused transient rerun. Then defer affected tickets if needed. |
| `amend_plan` | Amend binding constraints only within delegated authority, run `check-plan`, and continue. Local regrouping, independent-step ordering, and small guide overruns are ordinary adaptations, recorded in execution state without wave approval. Physical spec drift invokes `specifying-technical-implementation` with `pass: execution-patch` and exact parent-authorized files; the worker returns scoped results without commits, nested agents, versions, changelogs, or a PR. |
| `escalate_wave_lead` | Report shared or out-of-authority change; hold only affected dependent work and await the ruling. Invalid for self-planned execution or a direct single-epic wave with no separate wave lead. |
| `stop_lane` | Stops the epic with a blocker report in the final Epic report; the PR opens with what is done. |
| `resolve_and_rereview` | Assigns a fresh fix worker, then independent verification of the changed patch and interactions plus owner-required merge gates. |
| `escalate_strategy` | Changes approach instead of stopping; see `pr-review-fix-loop`. |
| `switch_to_fallback` | Moves the affected roles in `budget.fallback_models` to their approved models for budget pressure or unavailability, records it, and continues. `pr_reviewer` supplies replacements for panel entries while preserving independence. |

The policy has exactly one entry per trigger; `check-plan` errors on missing or duplicated triggers. Routine in-envelope changes don't trigger escalation. Resolve task decisions during step 0 or through delegated authority; actual host permission still applies.

{% if harness.id == "grok" %}In plan mode the wave lead may draft the whole wave in the session plan: each plan starts at a line `<!-- plan: EPIC-<ID> -->` followed by its own front matter and body. A bundle file in the repo is optional. `check-plan` validates a bundle plan by plan and across plans (sibling names, unique merge positions) once that file is written after exit. After approval each plan is copied unchanged to its own file.{% elif harness.id == "codex" or harness.id == "antigravity" %}The wave lead drafts the whole wave in one bundle file: each plan starts at a line `<!-- plan: EPIC-<ID> -->` followed by its own front matter and body, and `check-plan` validates such a bundle plan by plan and across plans (sibling names, unique merge positions). After approval each plan is copied unchanged to its own file.{% else %}In plan mode the wave lead drafts the whole wave in one file: each plan starts at a line `<!-- plan: EPIC-<ID> -->` followed by its own front matter and body, and `check-plan` validates such a bundle plan by plan and across plans (sibling names, unique merge positions). After approval each plan is copied unchanged to its own file.{% endif %}

## Body sections

### Repository state

What exists, what is partial, and what is missing for each ticket at `planned_at_commit`, with paths. This is the planner's inspection, and the source of every milestone's assertions below. Cite files, not impressions.

### Binding envelope

State the outcomes, acceptance, owned scope, contracts, dependencies, resource ceiling, permitted actions, and merge authority. Cite authoritative records and user rulings. Distinguish hard constraints from sizing and scheduling guides. A wave lead owns genuinely shared judgment; the epic lead owns execution inside this envelope. {% if harness.id == "codex" %}A single-epic wave uses the direct path when its epic lead is Codex. An approved Claude epic lead uses `orchestrated` invocation under the Codex wave lead, which retains merge/reconcile duties.{% else %}A single-epic wave runs directly as epic lead and retains wave merge/reconcile duties without another lead session.{% endif %}

### Milestones

An ordered list. Each milestone carries:

- **id** in the form `M1`, `M2`, and so on, and a one-line goal.
- **tickets**: the ids it completes, in ticket order.
- **acceptance**: ticket mode and exact evidence pointers. Use relevant source sections rather than copying full records into every worker prompt. Prefer decisive real-interface behavior proof and existing coverage; new isolated unit tests are rare exceptions, with no test-per-ticket or test-first quota.
- **verification**: focused implementation checks and the final required gate set, copied from declared project commands. Name who runs each and when source freezes. Leads and reviewers reuse valid results for unchanged inputs, adding checks only for uncovered risk or a mandatory project rule. Do not put final `live_probe` capture in an implementation milestone; capture once per epic after the last milestone. A run-mode ticket names its minimal evidence directory.
- **scope**: in-scope paths and out-of-scope paths for the worker, including the `artifact_path` of any contract or data-model artifact it creates or changes, and the tech-spec records and flow slugs it must follow.
- **repository_state**: relevant preconditions checkable by path or command. The epic lead checks changed inputs, distinguishes ordinary adaptation from binding drift, and reuses still-valid readiness on resume.
- **approach**: constraints the planner sets that the epic file does not, such as an ordering inside the milestone, a pattern to follow from a named existing file, or a thing not to do. Keep it to constraints. How the worker implements is the worker's.
- **expected_spec_patches**: tech-spec files the planner already expects physical drift in, so the lead can budget the extra commit and review. Expectation does not waive the spec-patch milestone.
- **independent_of**: milestone ids that may continue while this one is blocked or drifting, or none.
- **pilot**: `true` only for an uncertain prerequisite, with its decisive proof and dependent work to hold. Independent work continues.

Roughly 30 files or 1,500 lines is a sizing guide, not an escalation threshold. Review each coherent milestone's combined base/head delta once; incidental commits don't each create another gate. Preserve independent milestone coverage and separate code/spec commits. Small related stage changes can share a PR.

### Step-0 rulings

Record sourced preferences, user authorization, and rulings, including merge authority, whether an out-of-band epic waits for a running wave, and autonomy overrides. Cite this section on continuation instead of asking again. A task amendment doesn't change actual host permissions.

### Cross-lane facts

Conventions or identifiers this epic touches that a sibling also touches by reference: what it is, which sibling, and which lane decides. Empty when the wave is one or nothing is shared.

### Operational readiness

Record service prerequisites, launch/doctor commands, expected startup, observable progress, cleanup, and owner-required hosted blockers. Establish runtime/tool capabilities once and reuse until they change. Include worker and reviewer fallbacks without weakening independent review. Distinguish product failures, jobs that never start, and advancing gates; never launch duplicate work to obtain status.

Private projects verify locally unless the owner needs remote tests. Projects accepting outside contributions ordinarily use contribution CI; visibility alone doesn't establish the contribution model. Keep tests separate from release authentication, signing, publishing, and deployment. Existing owner-required gates bind the run; don't invent an arbitrary green-CI condition.

### Project runtime rules

Point to relevant sections for shared resources, locks, ports, service lifecycle, and required manual verification. Pass each worker focused startup pointers, revision, constraints, and expected proof. Reserve `verbatim_project_rules` for essential wording; don't copy background or entire ticket/spec records.

### Execution state

At a handoff, retain sourced preferences and rulings, authorized continuation, mode/caller/authority, stop boundary, current milestone, next gate, valid proof, and native agent/session/command handles. Record material local adaptations here. Derive mechanical status from existing tasks, commits, reports, and native events; don't maintain a second manual ledger. Where supported, distinguish queued, delivered, and applied messages. Restore this state and recheck only invalidated readiness.

### Amendments

Binding amendments belong to the delegated owner: the wave lead for shared changes, or the epic lead within its explicit amendment authority. Date each with its source report/ruling, action, changed constraints, and affected milestones. Keep ordinary adaptations in execution state. Record adopted interim assumptions and re-read applicable amendments on continuation.
